@meta
id: 23-03
title: Drill — start the word, let vim finish it
chapter: 23
type: drill
skills: complete-keyword
par: 25
minutes: 4
reps: 8
vscode: type three letters, press Tab
open: index.ts

@brief
Eight times: add `refundOrderOutsideWindow` to the end of the
`commands` object, after `cancelOrder`. Type no more than the first
seven letters of it.

@keys
| `Ctrl-N` | complete from the buffer |
| `ea` | append after the end of the current word |

@hints
`G$2b` lands on `cancelOrder`. `ea` opens insert mode right after it.
--
Type `, refundO` and press `Ctrl-N`. The rest of the name is already
in the import above.

@solution
    G$2b ea, refundO<Ctrl-N><Esc> :wq<Enter>

Twenty-one keystrokes for a twenty-four letter identifier. The drill is
about not stopping to look: type enough letters to be unique, press
`Ctrl-N`, keep going. If the wrong word appears, `Ctrl-N` again cycles;
if nothing appears, you have a typo in the prefix.

@start index.ts
import { placeOrder } from './place-order';
import { cancelOrder } from './cancel-order';
import { refundOrderOutsideWindow } from './refund-order-outside-window';

export const commands = { placeOrder, cancelOrder };

@expected index.ts
import { placeOrder } from './place-order';
import { cancelOrder } from './cancel-order';
import { refundOrderOutsideWindow } from './refund-order-outside-window';

export const commands = { placeOrder, cancelOrder, refundOrderOutsideWindow };
